'''blatter''' (blat′ėr), ''n.'' [< ''blatter, v.''] '''1.''' A rattling or clattering noise (as of boards falling).—'''2.''' A volley of clattering words.

'''blatterer''' (blat′ėr-ėr), ''n.'' One who blatters; a noisy blustering boaster.

'''blattering''' (blat′ėr-ing), ''n.'' [Verbal n. of ''blatter, v.''] Senseless blustering.

'''blatteroon†''' (blat-ẹ-rön′), ''n.'' [< L. ''blatero(n-)'', a babbler, < ''blaterare'', babble: see ''blatter.''] A senseless babbler.
{{smaller block|I trusted T. P. with a weighty secret, conjuring him that it should not take air and go abroad, . . . but it went out of him the very next day. . . . I hate such ''blatteroons.''
{{right|''Howell'', Letters, ii. 75.}}}}

'''Blattidæ''' (blat′i-dē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Blatta''<sup>1</sup> + ''-idæ.''] A family of cursorial orthopterous insects, the cockroaches, coextensive with the division ''Blattina'' or suborder ''Cursoria'', or even the order ''Dictyoptera.'' {{smaller|They have a flattened, lengthened, ovate body, with head retracted into the large shield-like prothorax; long, filamentous, many-jointed antennæ; long, strong cursorial legs, with setose tibiæ; 5-jointed tarsi, with an accessory joint or plantula between the claws; large coriaceous fore wings which overlap, and longitudinal folded hind wings, both sometimes undeveloped in females. The genera, species, and individuals are numerous, and are found in all parts of the world. Some attain a very large size in the tropics. They are mostly nocturnal, or live in dark places, and most of them are}}

